# ============================================================================
# P01 - I/O SCHEDULER: CFQ -> DEADLINE        [EXPERIMENTAL / UNVERIFIED]
# ============================================================================
# WHAT IT CHANGES
#   Default block I/O scheduler: CFQ -> Deadline. Also stops building CFQ.
#
#   baseline evidence (kernel source, commit 9b1c9a53):
#     block/Kconfig.iosched:22  config IOSCHED_DEADLINE
#     block/Kconfig.iosched:23          tristate "Deadline I/O scheduler"
#     block/Kconfig.iosched:24          default y
#     block/Kconfig.iosched:26  config IOSCHED_CFQ
#     block/Kconfig.iosched:27          tristate "CFQ I/O scheduler"
#     block/Kconfig.iosched:28          default y
#     block/Kconfig.iosched:44  choice
#     block/Kconfig.iosched:45          prompt "Default I/O scheduler"
#     block/Kconfig.iosched:46          default DEFAULT_CFQ
#     block/Kconfig.iosched:61  config DEFAULT_IOSCHED
#     block/Kconfig.iosched:62          default "deadline" if DEFAULT_DEADLINE
#     block/Kconfig.iosched:63          default "cfq" if DEFAULT_CFQ
#   Neither MT6765 defconfig mentions IOSCHED_*, so both take the Kconfig
#   defaults => CFQ is compiled in and is the default scheduler.
#   CFQ's own help text in this tree calls itself "the default I/O scheduler"
#   and describes per-process bandwidth distribution - a desktop-era design.
#
# WHY IT SHOULD HELP ON A 4 GB MT6765V
#   CFQ maintains a service tree per process plus group scheduling, and does
#   timestamped fairness accounting on every dispatch. On a phone the storage
#   is UFS/eMMC (flash, not rotational media), where fairness between
#   processes buys little, but the per-dispatch work is still paid. Deadline
#   is described in the same Kconfig as "simple and compact" and does CSCAN
#   plus FIFO expiry - materially less per-request bookkeeping.
#   The plausible benefit is therefore CPU time and power spent in the block
#   layer, which on a 4 GB Helio G99 competes directly with the 6x A55s that
#   Android background work lives on. It is a POWER and CPU-accounting
#   argument. It is NOT an argument that CFQ slows storage down - that is not
#   established by anything written here.
#
# WHAT WOULD VERIFY OR REFUTE IT
#   cat /sys/block/sda/queue/scheduler          (expect [deadline])
#   fio 4k randread + randwrite, baseline vs patched: IOPS AND, critically,
#     the delta in /proc/stat 'cpu' user+sys seconds spent during the run.
#     Faster-but-same-IOPS = a win. Same CPU time = no win, drop the patch.
#   dumpsys gfxinfo <pkg> framestats  (app cold-start / scroll, PSI io)
#   cat /proc/pressure/io
#   Watch for REGRESSION: foreground app launch under concurrent background
#     write load. Deadline can starve a single foreground reader more readily
#     than CFQ. If p50/p95 app-start regresses, this patch is a net loss.
#
# NOT PROPOSED HERE, DELIBERATELY
#   BFQ and Kyber are both BLK-MQ-only in this Kconfig (block/Kconfig.iosched
#   "MQ IOSCHED_KYBER", "IOSCHED_BFQ" are the mq variants) and I could NOT
#   confirm that MTK's MSDC block glue in this tree uses blk-mq - grepping
#   drivers/mmc/host/mtk-sd.c and drivers/mmc/host/mediatek/ComboA/{sd.c,
#   cmdq_hci.c} found no blk_mq_ops / blk_mq_init_queue. Until that is
#   confirmed, BFQ/Kyber are NOT offered. Gate to run before considering them:
#     grep -rn 'blk_mq_init_queue\|blk_alloc_disk\|blk_mq_ops' \
#          drivers/mmc/host/mtk-sd.c drivers/mmc/host/mediatek/ComboA/
#   Note also block/Makefile builds blk-mq.o unconditionally here (MTK dropped
#   the CONFIG_BLK_MQ gate), so the core exists - only the driver's use of it
#   is unconfirmed.
# ============================================================================

# CONFIG_IOSCHED_CFQ is not set
# CONFIG_CFQ_GROUP_IOSCHED is not set
CONFIG_DEFAULT_DEADLINE=y
CONFIG_DEFAULT_IOSCHED="deadline"

# Deadline is already default y in Kconfig; stated explicitly so the fragment
# is self-documenting and survives an upstream Kconfig default change.
CONFIG_IOSCHED_DEADLINE=y
